{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_001 answers the panel-vs-expert comparison but never addresses the problem's second, decisive requirement: identify the evidence within a single evaluation that distinguishes a genuine reliability gain from mere checklist compliance that happens to track the right answer. Its mechanism is all about LLN/variance reduction and 'expert blindness,' and its counterconditions are about when the panel fails, not about how to tell a real gain from coincidental compliance in the data you actually have. A counterexample: an evaluation where the panel scores higher on the objective outcomes exactly as SRC_001 predicts, yet every item was solved by rote box-ticking that tracked the key without the panel understanding why. Under SRC_001's own definitions this would count as the panel outperforming the expert, so it cannot separate the genuine gain the problem asks about from the compliant mimicry it warns against.","target":"194ee9db21a270b7c4abb5b02822a42a4fc70ea2285a42a224cdfa69fea99bc4"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_002's entire case rests on a 'Checklist Paradox' keyed to checklist size/cognitive load: as checklists grow, non-experts blind-tick and compliance only tracks the answer in simple cases, failing in complex ones. But that causal story is not invariant to checklist design. A short, high-usability checklist (explicitly named in its own countercondition) can produce reliable tracking on complex cases without inducing load-driven blind ticking; or, conversely, a long checklist can fail on simple cases if a step is misread. So the claimed mapping 'complex -> compliance failure' does not follow from the problem's inputs and is not a genuine basis for arguing against the panel. The distinguishing evidence it offers (fail in atypical cases) is conditional on an unsupported load mechanism rather than on the reliability-vs-compliance distinction the problem asks about.","counterexample":[{"expert":"intuition errs","outcome":"panel reliability is genuine on a complex case, falsifying SRC_002's 'complex -> catastrophic compliance failure' load-based claim","panel":"non-experts verify each criterion correctly and match the answer","task":"objectively-scored complex case with a low-load checklist"}],"target":"95e16f8eae56944c32dbe44952fcce03d56e6aa26500f5c87731d02142f6dffd"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_03 identifies 'discrepancy-driven discovery' (finding unlisted errors) as the marker of genuine gain. But this criterion is both too strong and misaligned with the problem's requirement. It is too strong because genuine reliability can occur under perfect checklist compliance with no unlisted-variable discovery: if the checklist exhaustively captures the objective verification criteria and the panel applies it correctly, reliability is real even though every error found was prompted. SRC_03's own countercondition (exhaustive checklist) exposes this, yet the target offers no fallback marker. It is also misaligned because the problem asks for evidence within a single evaluation, while 'discrepancy-driven discovery' is a process-level behavior whose absence does not establish mere compliance, nor whose presence is necessary for genuine gain. Thus it fails to provide the distinguishing evidence the problem demands.","counterexample":[{"expert":"lower accuracy","panel":"100% compliant, finds only listed errors, high accuracy","single_evaluation_evidence":"no discrepancy-driven discovery, yet reliability gain is genuine","src_03_verdict":"would classify as 'mere compliance' because only listed errors found, despite genuine gain","task":"objectively verifiable with an exhaustive checklist"}],"target":"e0478eea631cd8c37b2ff8b1f7e64e835e5b71c5b5048331387aeda916636aeb"}]}